{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"ROUND 1: BLOCK, one finding, ACCEPTED after verifying it in source.\n\nTHE FINDING: assert_gated hardcodes logging.getLogger(\"rebar.llm.config\") at config.py:230. Move the function to gate_context.py and that logger identity goes stale — the REBAR_GATE_ALLOW_UNGATED override warning would be attributed to a module that no longer contains the code.\n\nVERIFIED, and the verification changes how it should be fixed rather than whether: `grep -rn '\"rebar.llm.config\"' src/ tests/` returns exactly ONE line — that call site. No test filters on the logger name, no other source reference, no caplog assertion. So nothing BREAKS; the defect is purely observability, and re-pointing the logger is safe rather than risky.\n\nWHY IT STILL EARNS AN AC RATHER THAN AN INCIDENTAL EDIT: the warning it emits is security-relevant and the message itself calls the override 'audited'. An audited warning filed under the wrong module sends whoever greps the logs to a file that does not contain the code. That is worth an explicit criterion and a test, not a silent fix-up during a relocation.\n\nSWEPT THE CLASS BEFORE RESUBMITTING, per the lesson d8ef taught over five rounds. Searched the entire moved range (config.py:51-152 and :190-269) for identity-bearing literals: getLogger, __name__, __module__, quoted 'rebar.' strings and ContextVar names. Four hits — the one hardcoded logger, plus the three ContextVar debug names (rebar_llm_code_root and siblings). The ContextVar names stay ACCURATE after the move because the code remains under rebar.llm, and they are repr/debug only, so they need no change. The class is therefore limited to the single logger, and the new AC covers it.\n\nTwo defect classes had already been fixed on this ticket PRE-EMPTIVELY, before the first review: an out-of-repo AC requiring evidence in a Gerrit commit message and a ticket comment, and an AC asserting config.py 'has no such guard today' that sibling d8ef was about to falsify. Those two did not appear in this review's findings, which suggests the pre-emptive pass was worth doing."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785573946965267001,"uuid":"f72b3d48-4729-4ddb-a085-086739c2bf00"}